        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the auction and then win at least 61 points in tricks (out of 120 total card points).',
          gameplay: "1. **Dealing**: Each player receives 9 cards. 2. **Auction**: Players bid for the right to become the declarer. Bids include 'Game' (choosing trump), 'Solo' (playing without the skat), and 'Grand' (only Obers are trump). 3. **Trump Selection**: The declarer chooses the trump suit. 4. **Play**: Must follow suit. Must trump if void in led suit. 5. **Scoring**: Card points are counted. The declarer needs 61+ to win.",
          keyRules: [
            '36-card deck: 6-10, Under, Ober, King, Ace in four suits.',
            'Card ranks: Ace (11), Ten (10), King (4), Ober (3), Under (2), 9-8-7-6 (0).',
            'Total points per hand: 120.',
            "Auction: Bid 'Game', 'Solo', or 'Grand'.",
            "Declarer's goal: Win 61+ points.",
            'Must follow suit; must trump if void.',
            'Obers and Unters are permanent trumps in some contracts.',
            'Schneider (91+ points) and Schwarz (all tricks) bonuses apply.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Count points, not tricks. Aces and Tens are the valuable cards. Don't waste your high trumps on low-value tricks.",
          intermediate: "Trump Management: In a suit game, you have 11 trumps. Use them to capture opponents' Aces and Tens, but don't exhaust them too early.",
          advanced: "Void Creation: If you are declarer and have a weak suit, try to void yourself in that suit early so you can trump opponents' leads.",
          tips: [
            'The Ten is the second-highest card in point value but ranks low in trick-taking power.',
            'Schneider (91+ points) doubles the game value. Play for it if your hand is strong.',
            'Watch for opponents playing high non-trump cards earlyâ€”they may be voiding a suit.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
